  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 931 AM MDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nA passing upper-level trough in the northern tier of the US is \nsplitting the upper-level high in two today, with the western\nportion of high pressure redeveloping over the desert southwest. \nNortheasterly flow surrounding this new high pressure system will \ndirect storm motion to the southwest beginning this afternoon. \nSufficient moisture and lift from the passing trough will give the\nentire region a chance of thunderstorms. Area mountains have the \nbest chance of scattered thunderstorms (30-60%) and the lowlands \nhave an isolated chance for thunderstorms (20-40%) starting this \nafternoon and well into the overnight hours. Localized flash \nflooding and gusty outflow winds are the main hazards with any \nthunderstorms that develop, especially up in the mountains.\n\nConditions for Thursday will be very similar to today, though as the \nnew upper-level high strengthens over Arizona and New Mexico, storm \ncoverage is expected to slightly decrease for the lowlands on \nThursday. This does not mean the risk of localized flash flooding \nand gusty outflow winds will be gone on Thursday.\n\nDrier, continental air will mix in on Friday, leading to a downtick \nin storm activity for the lowlands. Storms will mainly be limited to \nthe area mountains and the western edge of New Mexico on both Friday \nand Saturday. Friday and Saturday are also expected to be the \nhottest days of the week, with lowland temperatures running up to 10 \ndegrees above average both days.\n\nA new ridge is forecast to set up in the southeastern United States \non Sunday, and both the GFS and ECMWF have a plume of moisture on \nthe west edge of the ridge heading into our region. Both model runs \nhave consistent moisture in our area from Sunday through the middle \nof next week. How much rain the region will get by mid-next week is \nuncertain as of now.",
